[{:db/id 17592186124638 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-fb2bfb86-4e76-5321-aed7-06eaaf8188f8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124636 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-75318d12-00c4-544e-97a9-126ab4836587" "question-f8d614db-1f17-5013-b1ed-45363a374722"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124194 :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero, and\n\n- the expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n![](images/5b/5b534c95f0105645223836a1b44f38db3f14bf61425b6f53ee7815742a86a068.svg)\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\big\\{ (x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,:\\, y \\geq -1, \\: y \\neq -x \\big\\}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124640 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7303070a-5c11-542d-9464-8fd77a6c4c78" "question-04c36299-59f0-54ea-b2d4-6a2a60540beb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124198 :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive,\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero, and\n\n- the expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y>0 \\\\ x>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\big\\{(x, y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,:\\, 2x+y >0, \\: x > 0 \\big\\}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124195 :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive, and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n![](images/68/68197b31685c209b98416f8e137dca514d7fbbf382d400688d411fa633ffd3c0.svg)\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD = \\left \\{ (x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\, : \\, y \\gt -x, \\, y \\neq 1-x \\right \\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we plot the corresponding domain. \n\n- Starting with $y> -x,$ we draw a dashed line $y=-x$ and shade the region *above* the line.\n\n- Then, we draw a dashed line $y=1-x$ through the shaded region.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/6a/6aa1aaeadb177cf5a80bcb12a247725cb3396586e7516c3ba30b46b03a9d2612.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-fb2bfb86-4e76-5321-aed7-06eaaf8188f8" :question/id #uuid "fb2bfb86-4e76-5321-aed7-06eaaf8188f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-158582" :question/problem "Evaluate $z=\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}$ at the point $(3,-\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(3,-\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ we substitute $x=3$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\,{|}_{(3,-\\pi /3)} & =\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}\\,{|}_{(3,-\\pi /3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0f239717-0131-5270-be88-b5cd8017e7fd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0f239717-0131-5270-be88-b5cd8017e7fd" :answer-field/id #uuid "0f239717-0131-5270-be88-b5cd8017e7fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d978482b-dadc-5142-91af-61294dc15739" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1c9017c3-0305-5a1f-91b7-5ff0abefd2b7" "answer-9cf5fa64-468b-5aa3-b046-e9ca3d40c1fc" "answer-4cdfbe0f-7325-5f77-a8c6-761b1ce65557" "answer-81903d38-806f-5011-865e-1ba6f995e0e0" "answer-d978482b-dadc-5142-91af-61294dc15739"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c9017c3-0305-5a1f-91b7-5ff0abefd2b7" :answer/id #uuid "1c9017c3-0305-5a1f-91b7-5ff0abefd2b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9cf5fa64-468b-5aa3-b046-e9ca3d40c1fc" :answer/id #uuid "9cf5fa64-468b-5aa3-b046-e9ca3d40c1fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4cdfbe0f-7325-5f77-a8c6-761b1ce65557" :answer/id #uuid "4cdfbe0f-7325-5f77-a8c6-761b1ce65557" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81903d38-806f-5011-865e-1ba6f995e0e0" :answer/id #uuid "81903d38-806f-5011-865e-1ba6f995e0e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d978482b-dadc-5142-91af-61294dc15739" :answer/id #uuid "d978482b-dadc-5142-91af-61294dc15739" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-75318d12-00c4-544e-97a9-126ab4836587" :question/id #uuid "75318d12-00c4-544e-97a9-126ab4836587" :question/math-academy-id "q-158551" :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x+y}{xy}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\nxy\\ne 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,xy\\ne 0\\}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-572ec305-a046-531a-8cd4-f40c72ec433f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-572ec305-a046-531a-8cd4-f40c72ec433f" :answer-field/id #uuid "572ec305-a046-531a-8cd4-f40c72ec433f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-53e832d0-cd61-540c-b2a3-7c2559bf84d5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bd074967-2295-5b21-8825-9badd7925df2" "answer-fb9c1db5-2655-5685-a7cd-b1d30f8510c3" "answer-53e832d0-cd61-540c-b2a3-7c2559bf84d5" "answer-fe8cd5f3-6aeb-530a-b211-e5b21f320134" "answer-9e4b6c86-9cf0-5ab0-9ffb-3dafec8612d7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd074967-2295-5b21-8825-9badd7925df2" :answer/id #uuid "bd074967-2295-5b21-8825-9badd7925df2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,xy=1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb9c1db5-2655-5685-a7cd-b1d30f8510c3" :answer/id #uuid "fb9c1db5-2655-5685-a7cd-b1d30f8510c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,xy>0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-53e832d0-cd61-540c-b2a3-7c2559bf84d5" :answer/id #uuid "53e832d0-cd61-540c-b2a3-7c2559bf84d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,xy\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fe8cd5f3-6aeb-530a-b211-e5b21f320134" :answer/id #uuid "fe8cd5f3-6aeb-530a-b211-e5b21f320134" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,xy\\ne 1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9e4b6c86-9cf0-5ab0-9ffb-3dafec8612d7" :answer/id #uuid "9e4b6c86-9cf0-5ab0-9ffb-3dafec8612d7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The domain is the entire $xy-$plane" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f8d614db-1f17-5013-b1ed-45363a374722" :question/id #uuid "f8d614db-1f17-5013-b1ed-45363a374722" :question/math-academy-id "q-158552" :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}+1\\ne 0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{3}\\ne -1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{3}\\ne -1\\}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c3b045b8-bf15-51b1-816f-a65dbb6c4b2e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c3b045b8-bf15-51b1-816f-a65dbb6c4b2e" :answer-field/id #uuid "c3b045b8-bf15-51b1-816f-a65dbb6c4b2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-00ed170e-f9ef-581a-a28f-e895638d17f2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a0dbcdf0-68f4-5a84-9587-f3fa35d6aba8" "answer-f87318a8-3643-5bd8-8b57-84f2fc51ba95" "answer-00ed170e-f9ef-581a-a28f-e895638d17f2" "answer-dcdecbfa-f590-53c6-baa7-254b9858e4a2" "answer-6dfe18bb-7b8e-5e23-b6bb-c3d39e3896af"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0dbcdf0-68f4-5a84-9587-f3fa35d6aba8" :answer/id #uuid "a0dbcdf0-68f4-5a84-9587-f3fa35d6aba8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{3}=-1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f87318a8-3643-5bd8-8b57-84f2fc51ba95" :answer/id #uuid "f87318a8-3643-5bd8-8b57-84f2fc51ba95" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{3}>-1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00ed170e-f9ef-581a-a28f-e895638d17f2" :answer/id #uuid "00ed170e-f9ef-581a-a28f-e895638d17f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{3}\\ne -1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dcdecbfa-f590-53c6-baa7-254b9858e4a2" :answer/id #uuid "dcdecbfa-f590-53c6-baa7-254b9858e4a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{3}\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6dfe18bb-7b8e-5e23-b6bb-c3d39e3896af" :answer/id #uuid "6dfe18bb-7b8e-5e23-b6bb-c3d39e3896af" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The domain is the entire $xy-$plane" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7303070a-5c11-542d-9464-8fd77a6c4c78" :question/id #uuid "7303070a-5c11-542d-9464-8fd77a6c4c78" :question/math-academy-id "q-158565" :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{x+y}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- $\\sqrt{x+y}$ is defined for $x$ and $y,$ such that $x+y\\ge 0,$ and\n\n- $\\operatorname{cos}⁡g(x,y)$ is defined if $g(x,y)$ is defined.\n\nAs a result, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{x+y}$ is defined for values of $x$ and $y$ that satisfy $x+y\\ge 0.$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x+y\\ge 0\\}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-eb150068-ffd1-5d6f-a433-79938dc22c47"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-eb150068-ffd1-5d6f-a433-79938dc22c47" :answer-field/id #uuid "eb150068-ffd1-5d6f-a433-79938dc22c47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ffc86b0d-b945-5389-9124-0ca53da17c91" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9b14ad04-2e98-56ed-947e-c11c491e233f" "answer-76429727-c36c-5953-99b6-ad256b53ad35" "answer-ffc86b0d-b945-5389-9124-0ca53da17c91" "answer-cd1e66fe-8752-50b3-95bb-5ddf851dbaeb" "answer-d58e238a-cef0-5f2c-af19-539f41d9c5c8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9b14ad04-2e98-56ed-947e-c11c491e233f" :answer/id #uuid "9b14ad04-2e98-56ed-947e-c11c491e233f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<x+y<2\\pi \\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-76429727-c36c-5953-99b6-ad256b53ad35" :answer/id #uuid "76429727-c36c-5953-99b6-ad256b53ad35" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x+y>0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ffc86b0d-b945-5389-9124-0ca53da17c91" :answer/id #uuid "ffc86b0d-b945-5389-9124-0ca53da17c91" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x+y\\ge 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd1e66fe-8752-50b3-95bb-5ddf851dbaeb" :answer/id #uuid "cd1e66fe-8752-50b3-95bb-5ddf851dbaeb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x+y\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d58e238a-cef0-5f2c-af19-539f41d9c5c8" :answer/id #uuid "d58e238a-cef0-5f2c-af19-539f41d9c5c8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The domain is the entire $xy$-plane" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-04c36299-59f0-54ea-b2d4-6a2a60540beb" :question/id #uuid "04c36299-59f0-54ea-b2d4-6a2a60540beb" :question/math-academy-id "q-158583" :question/problem "Find the domain of the function  $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{y-x}).$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The argument of the arccosine has values between $-1$ and $1,$ and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-1<\\frac{1}{y-x}<1 \\\\ y-x\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-1\\le \\frac{1}{y-x}\\le 1,\\,y-x\\ne 0\\}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4261be1e-d3be-5915-93ea-32ad8e744bfc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4261be1e-d3be-5915-93ea-32ad8e744bfc" :answer-field/id #uuid "4261be1e-d3be-5915-93ea-32ad8e744bfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9d38eb2d-c186-5118-a6d9-96fbee67928e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9d38eb2d-c186-5118-a6d9-96fbee67928e" "answer-e3684b28-fb6d-5385-b110-85d8de2e97a6" "answer-6328591f-26cd-5a9c-bb16-a094d0fce559" "answer-9ea63572-0b73-594a-9be3-31c1af1775f0" "answer-43ccfe2f-0560-528d-ba84-ade4a43c6a21"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9d38eb2d-c186-5118-a6d9-96fbee67928e" :answer/id #uuid "9d38eb2d-c186-5118-a6d9-96fbee67928e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-1\\le \\frac{1}{y-x}\\le 1,\\,y-x\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e3684b28-fb6d-5385-b110-85d8de2e97a6" :answer/id #uuid "e3684b28-fb6d-5385-b110-85d8de2e97a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-1\\le \\frac{1}{y-x}\\le 1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6328591f-26cd-5a9c-bb16-a094d0fce559" :answer/id #uuid "6328591f-26cd-5a9c-bb16-a094d0fce559" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0\\le \\frac{1}{y-x}\\le \\pi \\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ea63572-0b73-594a-9be3-31c1af1775f0" :answer/id #uuid "9ea63572-0b73-594a-9be3-31c1af1775f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y-x\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-43ccfe2f-0560-528d-ba84-ade4a43c6a21" :answer/id #uuid "43ccfe2f-0560-528d-ba84-ade4a43c6a21" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The domain is the entire $xy$-plane" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124800 :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 1\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the inequality involves the $\\ge $ sign, the curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ is part of the domain. Therefore, we draw a solid curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and shade the area *outside* the curve.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/ba/ba31bbc16c58860a4bc3c4d33ea1a61cb3d86d0e73d8aa6a67869b3e96a7fe32.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186388995 :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0\\,⟹\\,(x,y)\\ne (0,0)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,(x.y)\\ne (0,0)\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the expression involves the $\\ne $ sign, the point $(0,0)$ is *not* part of the domain. Therefore, we exclude the point $(x,y)=(0,0)$ and shade the plane.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/70/701c54bff1c431883f21cb980ac43f4f2f6beabb11817066a4e909d5a2663a25.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125868 :tutorial/content "A function $f(x,y)$ of two variables is a rule that maps each pair of numbers $(x,y)\\in D$ to a unique real number. Under this notation, $D\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2$ is the **domain** of $f.$\n\nFor instance, consider the following two-variable function: \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\dfrac{\\sin(xy)}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this function by substituting concrete pairs of numbers for $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n- Let's evaluate $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2, \\pi).$ To do that, we substitute $x=2$ and $y=\\pi$ into the function expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,\\pi ) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)}{\\sqrt{x-1}}{|}_{(2,\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\pi )}{\\sqrt{2-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(2,\\pi)$ returned a unique real number, this means that $(2,\\pi)$ belongs to the domain of $f,$ i.e., $(2,\\pi)\\in D.$\n\n- Let's now try to evaluate $f(x,y)$ at the point $(0, \\pi).$ To do that, we substitute $x=0$ and $y=\\pi$ into the function expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,\\pi ) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)}{\\sqrt{x-1}}{|}_{(0,\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0\\cdot \\pi )}{\\sqrt{0-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{-1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined over the real numbers.\n\nSince $f(0,\\pi)$ did not return a unique *real* number, this means that $(0,\\pi)\\notin D.$\n\nWe can also have functions of three variables $f(x,y,z),$ functions of four variables, $f(x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4),$ etc. There is no limit to the number of variables a function can have. Functions with more than one variable are called **multivariable functions.**" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125865 :tutorial/content "Another way of visualizing a two-variable function is to draw its graph as a **surface** in a three-dimensional coordinate system. \n\nFor example, consider the function\n\n$$\nz = f(x,y) = x^2 +y^2, \\quad (x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the (restricted) domain is given by the unit disc \n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\: : \\: x^2+y^2 \\leq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $f$ is a surface that consists of all the points with coordinates\n\n$$\n(x, \\, y, \\, f(x,y)), \\quad (x,y) \\in D,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/a3/a3c9c7eca49a0f828e53c0775afac16335033c1a8881367e49098bf7125ee2e6.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that range of $f$ is represented by the interval $R=[0,1]$ on the vertical $z$-axis. It consists of the possible $z$-coordinates of the points on our surface." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124701 :activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
